<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bregenz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bregenz is the capital of the Austrian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a>, on the eastern shore of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. On the site of the Roman town of Brigantium, it was ruled by the counts of Bregenz and then of Montfort, who sold it in stages to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1451 and 1523. It hosts the Bregenz Festival, staged on a floating platform on the lake.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bregenz.jpg" alt="Bregenz" width="320" height="162" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Feel free to use my photos, but please mention me as the author and if you want send me a message. or (rufre@lenz-nenning.at) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bregenz_am_Morgen_vom_Seibel,_B%C3%B6hringer.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bregenz lies on the east and southeast shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, the third-largest freshwater lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> to the west and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> to the northwest, with the large forests of the Bregenzerwald to the east. It stands on a plateau falling in a series of terraces to the lake at the foot of the Pfänder mountain. It is a junction of the arterial roads from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> valley to the German Alpine foothills, and cruise ships run on the lake. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a>, of which it is the capital, is the westernmost state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first settlements date from about 1500 BCE. Strabo mentions the Brigantii as a Celtic sub-tribe of this region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and in the 5th century BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> settled at Brigantion, one of their most heavily fortified places. After a series of battles in 15 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> conquered it and it became a Roman camp, and around 50 CE it was made a municipality, Brigantium. It was the seat of the Roman admiralty for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> and had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. The Alemanni destroyed it in 259/60 and settled the area around 450. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columbanus">Saint Columbanus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-gall">Saint Gall</a> worked there as missionaries from 610 to 612.</p><p>From 917 the castle was the residence of the Udalrichinger, the ruling dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vorarlberg">Vorarlberg</a>, who called themselves Counts of Bregenz; the house died out around 1150. Around 1170 Hugo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, called Montfort, founded a town settlement, first documented in 1249. The city was sold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in 1451 and again in 1523 and stayed under Austrian rule, apart from a brief occupation by Swedish forces under Carl Gustaf Wrangel in the 30 Years' War, until the 19th century. The Swedes destroyed the fortress of Hohenbregenz on the Gebhardsberg in 1647. Bregenz was under Bavarian rule from 1805 to 1814, and since 1726 it has been the main seat of Austrian administration in Vorarlberg.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The harbour on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a> was built from 1842 to 1850, Austrian ship service began in 1884, and railway services have existed since 1872. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> bombed the town in 1945, destroying 72 houses, and French troops set up the military government in April and May of that year. The Bregenz Festival, with the Vienna Symphony Orchestra, began in 1946 and is held every summer, with a stage floating on the lake. The upper town keeps remains of its walls, the old town hall of 1662 and Martin's Tower, originally built by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> steeple is the largest bulb-shaped one in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>. The Kunsthaus Bregenz, a modern art museum, was built from 1991 to 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bregenz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their League above the Lake briefly spread across eastern Switzerland before its defeat at <b>Bregenz</b> in 1408.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-appenzell">Canton of Appenzell</a></li><li><blockquote>His descendants divided into several lines, including Werdenberg, Feldkirch, <b>Bregenz</b> and Tettnang, holding lands around the eastern shore of Lake Constance.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-montfort">County of Montfort</a></li></ul></section>
